WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.047
Workload identity represents software
rather than a human user.

2
00:00:04.047 --> 00:00:06.661
A platform can associate that identity
with

3
00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:09.181
a virtual machine, container, serverless
function,

4
00:00:09.181 --> 00:00:12.168
hosted application, Kubernetes workload,
or deployment job.

5
00:00:12.168 --> 00:00:14.968
The identity is not itself a secret.

6
00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:17.582
It is the principal and trust mechanism

7
00:00:17.582 --> 00:00:20.382
through which the workload obtains usable
credentials.

8
00:00:20.382 --> 00:00:22.622
The common flow starts with the

9
00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:24.676
platform establishing the workload's
identity.

10
00:00:24.676 --> 00:00:26.916
Application code asks a supported
credential

11
00:00:26.916 --> 00:00:29.717
provider for access to a target service.

12
00:00:29.717 --> 00:00:34.104
The platform issues or brokers a
short-lived credential, often a token.

13
00:00:34.104 --> 00:00:37.184
The client presents that credential, or
uses temporary

14
00:00:37.184 --> 00:00:40.451
signing credentials, and the receiving
service evaluates authorization.

15
00:00:40.451 --> 00:00:42.691
The application avoids carrying a
permanent

16
00:00:42.691 --> 00:00:45.118
cloud key through its deployment
configuration.

17
00:00:45.118 --> 00:00:47.545
Examples include cloud roles, managed
identities,

18
00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:49.692
service accounts, and federated
identities.

19
00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:53.519
With OpenID Connect federation, a trusted
issuer supplies an assertion

20
00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:57.813
that a receiving system validates and may
exchange for service credentials.

21
00:00:57.813 --> 00:01:01.920
Trust must constrain the expected issuer,
audience, and workload claims.

22
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.907
An assertion intended for one audience is
not

23
00:01:04.907 --> 00:01:08.081
automatically a valid access token for
every API.

24
00:01:08.081 --> 00:01:11.255
For Kubernetes, use workload-specific
service accounts, narrowly scoped

25
00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:14.148
permissions, and short-lived projected
tokens where supported.

26
00:01:14.148 --> 00:01:17.975
Access to cloud services requires an
appropriate trust integration; creating

27
00:01:17.975 --> 00:01:21.896
a Kubernetes service account alone does
not grant cloud authority.

28
00:01:21.896 --> 00:01:24.883
Protect the runtime and its credential
endpoint because

29
00:01:24.883 --> 00:01:28.430
compromised code may still obtain
credentials as the workload.

30
00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.510
Compared with permanent keys in
environment variables, this

31
00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.311
design reduces static distribution and
developer handling.

32
00:01:34.311 --> 00:01:36.644
Providers can renew credentials
automatically, and

33
00:01:36.644 --> 00:01:38.698
identity-specific audit records improve
attribution.

34
00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:41.778
Removing trust or permissions can stop
future access,

35
00:01:41.778 --> 00:01:44.392
but already issued credentials may remain
usable

36
00:01:44.392 --> 00:01:47.565
until expiration or until the service
enforces revocation.

37
00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:51.579
Short-lived does not mean harmless: tokens
remain sensitive while valid.

38
00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:54.473
Finally, identity establishes who is
requesting access.

39
00:01:54.473 --> 00:01:56.713
Narrow roles and resource permissions
still

40
00:01:56.713 --> 00:01:59.140
determine what that workload may do.

